## Summary of Exploratory Findings

Summarize the main findings from the exploratory analysis and justify the modeling approach.

**Key Findings**

The exploratory analysis identified positive relationships between PurchaseAmount and Income, PromoUsed, PagesViewed, and WebsiteVisits, while SatisfactionScore showed no clear linear association. Since the response variable is continuous and the relationships with the main predictors are approximately linear, multiple linear regression was selected as the appropriate modeling approach. The candidate predictors will be further evaluated during model development.



# Model Development

Develop and compare multiple linear regression models to identify the combination of predictors that best explains customer purchase amount.

## Baseline Model

Develop a simple linear regression model using the strongest individual predictor identified during the exploratory analysis.

**Model Equation**

$$
\text{PurchaseAmount} = \beta_0 + \beta_1 \times \text{Income}
$$

```{r}

# Fit the baseline regression model
baseline_model <- lm(
  PurchaseAmount ~ Income,
  data = marketing
)

# Display model summary
summary(baseline_model)

# Calculate confidence intervals
confint(baseline_model)

# Model selection criteria
AIC(baseline_model)
BIC(baseline_model)
```
**Key Findings**

Income was selected as the predictor for the baseline model because it showed the strongest linear relationship with PurchaseAmount during the exploratory analysis (r = 0.58). The model was statistically significant (p < 0.001) and explained approximately 34.0% of the variability in customer spending. Although this provides a useful starting point, the relatively modest explanatory power suggests that additional predictors may improve model performance.


## Multiple Regression Model

Develop a multiple linear regression model including all candidate predictors identified during the exploratory analysis.

**Model Equation**

$$
\text{PurchaseAmount}
=
\beta_0
+
\beta_1 \times \text{Income}
+
\beta_2 \times \text{WebsiteVisits}
+
\beta_3 \times \text{PagesViewed}
+
\beta_4 \times \text{PromoUsed}
+
\beta_5 \times \text{SatisfactionScore}
$$
```{r}

# Fit the multiple regression model
multiple_model <- lm(
  PurchaseAmount ~ Income +
    WebsiteVisits +
    PagesViewed +
    PromoUsed +
    SatisfactionScore,
  data = marketing
)

# Display model summary
summary(multiple_model)

# Calculate confidence intervals
confint(multiple_model)

# Model selection criteria
AIC(multiple_model)
BIC(multiple_model)
```
**Key Findings**

The multiple regression model substantially improved the model performance compared with the baseline model. The explained variance increased from 34.0% to 77.7%, indicating that customer spending is better explained when demographic and behavioral variables are considered together. Among the candidate predictors, SatisfactionScore was the only variable that was not statistically significant (p = 0.601), making it the first candidate for removal during model refinement.

## Refined Regression Model

Refine the multiple regression model by removing non-significant predictors while maintaining model performance.

**Model Equation**

$$
\text{PurchaseAmount}
=
\beta_0
+
\beta_1 \times \text{Income}
+
\beta_2 \times \text{WebsiteVisits}
+
\beta_3 \times \text{PagesViewed}
+
\beta_4 \times \text{PromoUsed}
$$

```{r}

# Fit the refined regression model
refined_model <- lm(
  PurchaseAmount ~ Income +
    WebsiteVisits +
    PagesViewed +
    PromoUsed,
  data = marketing
)

# Display model summary
summary(refined_model)

# Calculate confidence intervals
confint(refined_model)

# Model selection criteria
AIC(refined_model)
BIC(refined_model)
```
**Key Findings**

Removing SatisfactionScore simplified the regression model without reducing its explanatory power. The refined model maintained the same goodness of fit while achieving lower AIC and BIC values, indicating a more parsimonious model. Since all remaining predictors were statistically significant (p < 0.001), this model was selected as the final regression model.

## Model Comparison

Compare the regression models using goodness-of-fit and model selection criteria to identify the preferred model.

| Metric | Baseline | Multiple | Refined |
|:-------|---------:|---------:|--------:|
| Predictors | 1 | 5 | 4 |
| Multiple R² | 0.3399 | 0.7769 | 0.7769 |
| Adjusted R² | 0.3398 | 0.7767 | 0.7767 |
| Residual Std. Error | 17.43 | 10.13 | 10.13 |
| AIC | 42772.86 | 37356.49 | **37354.76** |
| BIC | 42792.42 | 37402.11 | **37393.87** |

**Key Findings**

The baseline model established a useful benchmark, explaining 34.0% of the variation in PurchaseAmount using Income alone. Adding behavioral variables substantially improved the model, increasing the explained variance to 77.7%. Removing the non-significant predictor (SatisfactionScore) reduced model complexity while maintaining the same predictive performance and improving both AIC and BIC. Therefore, the refined regression model was selected as the final model for subsequent analysis.


# Model Diagnostics

Evaluate whether the final regression model satisfies the key assumptions of multiple linear regression.

## Linearity

Assess whether the relationship between the predictors and the response variable is approximately linear.

```{r}

library(car)

# Residuals vs Fitted Values
plot(refined_model, which = 1)

# Component + Residual (Partial Residual) Plots
crPlots(refined_model)
```
**Key Findings**

The Residuals vs Fitted plot showed residuals randomly scattered around zero with no systematic pattern, supporting the assumption of linearity for the model as a whole. The Component + Residual plots indicated approximately linear relationships for each retained predictor (Income, WebsiteVisits, PagesViewed, and PromoUsed). Therefore, no substantial evidence of non-linearity was observed, and no corrective action was required.

## Normality of Residuals

Assess whether the residuals of the final regression model are approximately normally distributed.

```{r}

# Histogram of residuals
hist(
  residuals(refined_model),
  breaks = 30,
  main = "Histogram of Residuals",
  xlab = "Residuals",
  col = "lightblue",
  border = "white"
)

# Q-Q Plot
plot(refined_model, which = 2)

# Shapiro-Wilk Test
shapiro.test(residuals(refined_model))
```
**Key Findings**

The histogram and Q-Q plot indicated that the residuals were approximately normally distributed, with only minor deviations in the tails. The Shapiro-Wilk test also failed to reject the null hypothesis of normality (W = 0.9997, p = 0.7172), providing additional statistical evidence that the residuals follow a normal distribution. Therefore, the normality assumption was considered satisfied, and no corrective action was required.

## Homoscedasticity

Assess whether the residuals exhibit constant variance across the range of fitted values.

```{r}
library(lmtest)

# Scale-Location Plot
plot(refined_model, which = 3)

# Breusch-Pagan Test
bptest(refined_model)
```
**Key Findings**

The Scale-Location plot showed a relatively constant spread of residuals across the fitted values, with no evidence of a funnel-shaped pattern. The Breusch–Pagan test also failed to detect heteroscedasticity (BP = 2.38, p = 0.6663). Together, these results indicate that the homoscedasticity assumption was satisfied, and no corrective action was required.

## Multicollinearity

Assess whether the independent variables are highly correlated with one another, which could affect the stability of the regression coefficients.

```{r}
library(car)

vif(refined_model)
```
**Key Findings**

The Variance Inflation Factor (VIF) values for all retained predictors were approximately equal to 1, indicating virtually no multicollinearity. This suggests that each predictor contributes unique information to the model, resulting in stable coefficient estimates and reliable statistical inference. Therefore, no corrective action was required.

## Influential Observations (Cook's Distance)

Identify observations that exert an unusually large influence on the estimated regression model.

```{r}

plot(refined_model, which = 5)

cook <- cooks.distance(refined_model)

threshold <- 4 / nrow(marketing)

cat("Cook's Distance threshold:", round(threshold, 5), "\n")
cat("Maximum Cook's Distance:", round(max(cook), 5), "\n")
cat("Potentially influential observations:", sum(cook > threshold), "\n")
```
**Key Findings**

The Cook's Distance analysis identified 255 observations above the conservative screening threshold (4/n). However, the largest Cook's Distance was only 0.0038, indicating that no individual observation exerted excessive influence on the regression model. Therefore, all observations were retained, and no corrective action was required.

## Diagnostic Summary

Summarize the results of the regression diagnostic tests and assess whether the final model satisfies the assumptions of multiple linear regression.

| Assumption               | Diagnostic Method                                 | Result                          | Action Required |
| ------------------------ | ------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------- | --------------- |
| Linearity                | Residuals vs. Fitted & Component + Residual Plots | Satisfied                       | No              |
| Normality                | Histogram, Q-Q Plot & Shapiro-Wilk Test           | Satisfied                       | No              |
| Homoscedasticity         | Scale-Location Plot & Breusch-Pagan Test          | Satisfied                       | No              |
| Multicollinearity        | Variance Inflation Factor (VIF)                   | Not detected                    | No              |
| Influential Observations | Cook's Distance                                   | No excessive influence detected | No              |

**Key Findings**

The final regression model satisfied all major assumptions of multiple linear regression. Diagnostic plots and statistical tests supported the adequacy of the model, indicating linear relationships, approximately normal residuals, constant variance, no evidence of multicollinearity, and no excessively influential observations. The refined regression model was therefore retained as the final model for interpretation.

